module sh.sysl.ui

// Colour arithmetic, and why a toolkit needs any at all.
//
// **A style is asked for once and worn in three states**, which is the whole reason this file
// exists. A button is given one accent colour and has to look like something when the pointer is
// over it and like something else while it is held down; a caller made to name all three would
// spend most of a theme saying the same hue in slightly different words, and a caller who named
// only one would get no feedback at all.
//
// So the states are *derived*: hover is the accent moved toward white, pressed is the accent moved
// toward black, and a border is the ground moved a little further than either. That is what a
// designer does by hand in a palette, and it is arithmetic rather than judgement.
//
// A colour is `0xRRGGBB` in a `u32`, which is what `Canvas` takes and what an application writes as
// a hex literal. There is no alpha: nothing in the toolkit composites, and a channel nobody reads is
// a channel that goes wrong silently.

const WHITE: u32 = 0xFFFFFF
const BLACK: u32 = 0x000000

// The channel `shift` bits up, as a number from 0 to 255.
private channel(c: u32, shift: u32) -> int = int((c >> shift) & 0xFF)

// One channel of the answer. Integer arithmetic on purpose: the inputs are bytes and the output is a
// byte, so there is nothing for a float to be more accurate about.
private blend(x: int, y: int, p: int) -> int = (x * (100 - p) + y * p) / 100

private pack(r: int, g: int, b: int) -> u32 = (u32(r) << 16) | (u32(g) << 8) | u32(b)

// `a` at `pct` of nothing, `b` at `pct` of a hundred, and a straight line between them.
//
// **A percentage outside the range is clamped rather than refused**, because this is called from a
// paint pass with a number a style computed, and a user interface that stopped drawing over an
// arithmetic slip would be worse than one that drew the nearest colour it could.
mix(a: u32, b: u32, pct: int) -> u32
    val p = if pct < 0 then 0 elif pct > 100 then 100 else pct

    pack(blend(channel(a, 16), channel(b, 16), p),
         blend(channel(a, 8), channel(b, 8), p),
         blend(channel(a, 0), channel(b, 0), p))

// Toward white, and toward black.
//
// **These are a mix against a constant and nothing cleverer**, which is a choice rather than a
// shortcut: lightening in a perceptual space would be better and would need a colour space, a pair
// of transfer functions and a dependency on floating point. What a hover effect needs is that the
// colour visibly moves and that two colours moved by the same amount move by the same amount.
lighten(c: u32, pct: int) -> u32 = mix(c, WHITE, pct)

darken(c: u32, pct: int) -> u32 = mix(c, BLACK, pct)
